window.docContents[164].resolve({"/Foundations/Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Main-objects":{"contents":"Finite-dimensional space is Mathlib's Euclidean type .\nSpacetime points carry a distinguished time coordinate and a spatial\nprojection. Coordinate derivatives are derived from Mathlib's Fréchet\nderivative, and the familiar operators are finite coordinate constructions\nbuilt on top of it:\n\n\n\nThe coordinate identity  holds\nunconditionally for these total Lean expressions. Interpreting them as\nclassical derivatives requires regularity. In particular, local \nregularity identifies this iterated-coordinate Laplacian with Mathlib's\nLaplacian defined through the second Fréchet derivative.\n\nA locally integrable function \nacts on compactly supported smooth test functions  by integration,\n, and distributional differentiation pushes the\nderivative onto the test function with the usual sign:\n\n\n\nDistributional differentiation also gives meaning to derivatives of\ndiscontinuous functions. The standard illustration is the Heaviside step\nfunction on . It has no derivative at the origin in any pointwise\nsense, and yet its distributional derivative is the Dirac mass ,\nbecause  for every test function\n. PDE theory needs this much generality. Solutions of hyperbolic\nconservation laws grow jumps like that in finite time, and elliptic theory has\nto cope with forcing terms that are rough to begin with.\n\nThe second Green identity on a ball\nrelates the interior equations of two  functions to their values and\nnormal derivatives on the sphere. Gauss–Green extends this calculation to\nbounded  graph domains, using intrinsic surface measure and outward normals.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Operators, solution concepts, and weak formulations","header":"3.1.2. Main objects","id":"/Foundations/Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Main-objects"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Conservation-and-Incompressibility/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility":{"contents":"Particle paths and material derivatives turn local conservation of mass and\nmomentum into the Eulerian equations.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","header":"8.2. Conservation and Incompressibility","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Conservation-and-Incompressibility/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--The-Steady-Stokes-Problem":{"contents":"\n\nDropping the nonlinear term and the time derivative from the Navier–Stokes\nequations leaves the Stokes system\n\n\n\nat unit viscosity. The pressure is the Lagrange multiplier of the constraint\n.  treats the problem in this\nway, by minimizing the Dirichlet energy over divergence-free fields. The\nEuler–Lagrange equation determines the pressure as a multiplier.\n\nFor a domain , a force \nand , the Stokes energy iswith  the Frobenius norm of the full gradient matrix. The Stokes\nresidual of  is the vector of distributions , defined\nthrough the weak first derivatives of .\n\nLet  and let  be bounded. For every , there is\nexactly one  with  that\nminimizes  among all divergence-free fields in . The minimizer\nsatisfies\n\nThe divergence is a bounded linear map on , so its kernel is a closed\nsubspace and therefore a Hilbert space. On a bounded domain the Poincaré\ninequality makes the Dirichlet form coercive there, and the direct method for\na coercive symmetric form on a closed subspace gives a unique minimizer. The\nweak equation is the derivative of  at , which is\nadmissible because  stays divergence free.\n\nThe weak equation tests only against divergence-free fields. To recover\npressure, we factor the residual through the divergence of a general test\nfield. The constrained minimum supplies this factorization, and local\nregularity gives a measurable pressure representing the resulting functional.\n\nLet  be a divergence-free minimizer of  on an open set .\nThen the residual  is represented by a function that is square\nintegrable on every compact subset of , and every component\n of the vorticity has weak first derivatives\nthat are locally square integrable. No second derivative of  is assumed.\n\nTesting the weak equation against ,\nwhich is divergence free, shows that the residual is weakly curl free. Each\nvorticity component then satisfies\n in distributions, a Poisson\nequation whose right-hand side is a derivative of an  field, and the\ninterior estimate for such equations gives locally square integrable first\nderivatives. Because , the Laplacian of  is the\ndivergence of the vorticity tensor, which gives the local  representative\nof the residual.\n\nLet , let  be any open set, and let\n. Suppose \nis divergence free and minimizes  against every divergence-free\n competitor. There is a measurable pressure  that is square integrable on\nevery compact subset of , whose distributional gradient is\n, and for whichfor every  that vanishes outside a compact\nsubset of . The test field need not be divergence free.\nThe same pressure has an  representative on every open  with\ncompact closure contained in . Neither connectedness nor simple\nconnectedness is required.\n\nThe residual is a locally  field with zero weak curl, so it has weak\npotentials on balls. To make these potentials agree, use the algebraic\nfunctional obtained by factoring the first variation through divergence.\nOn a smaller coordinate box, the difference between that functional and\nintegration against a local potential annihilates all coordinate derivatives.\nIt is therefore a constant times the integral. Adding this constant to the\npotential makes it represent the prescribed functional.The adjusted potentials represent the same functional on overlaps and hence\nagree almost everywhere there. A countable open subcover gives a measurable\ngluing. Compact-local  bounds for both the pressure and its weak gradient\ngive its  representatives on relatively compact open subsets.\nThe identity for smooth compactly supported tests is the definition of the\ndistributional gradient.\nIt extends to compactly supported  tests by localizing the stress\n with a cutoff equal to one near the support of the test, so\nthat every integral involves only local  bounds.\n\nExistence of the minimizer uses boundedness through Poincaré's inequality.\nPressure recovery starts from an actual minimum on any open domain: the\nminimum provides the common algebraic potential that fixes the local\nconstants, and curl-freeness alone would not justify gluing on an arbitrary\ndomain.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow","header":"8.5.3. The Steady Stokes Problem","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--The-Steady-Stokes-Problem"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Weak-Compactness-for-the-Direct-Method--Weak-compactness-toolkit":{"contents":"For a complete real Hilbert space , the canonical isometric mapis surjective. Thus  is reflexive in the functional-analytic sense used\nby the direct method.\n\nPull a double-dual functional back along the Fréchet–Riesz isometric\nequivalence between the Hilbert space and its continuous dual. Applying the\ninverse equivalence produces a vector whose canonical double-dual image is the\noriginal functional.\n\nReflexivity feeds the compactness statements: it identifies the weak topology\nof  with the weak-star topology of its double dual, where Banach–Alaoglu\napplies. The norm-bound and Radon–Riesz estimates use something more\nelementary, namely the defining property of weak convergence.\n\nWeak convergence in a real normed space gives convergence against each continuous functional:\n\nThe weak topology on  is the initial topology generated by all continuous\nlinear functionals . Therefore each evaluation map is\ncontinuous by construction. Applying that continuity to\n gives\n for every .\n\nReflexivity and Banach–Alaoglu give the compactness half of the direct\nmethod: a norm bound on a minimizing sequence, together with weak closedness\nof the sublevel set it lives in, is enough to produce a weak cluster point.\n\nA weakly closed subset of a complete real Hilbert space is weakly compact when\nits underlying normed-space set is bounded:The Lean theorem is stated for Mathlib's weak-space topology and proves\ntopological compactness.\n\nHilbert reflexivity identifies the weak space with the range of its canonical\nweak-star double-dual embedding. Banach–Alaoglu gives compactness of the bounded\nweak-star closure, and the embedding transfers compactness back to the weak\ntopology. Weak closedness removes the closure operation.\n\nCompactness of a sublevel set does not by itself keep a weak limit inside a\nconstrained admissible class. For linear constraints, weak continuity of the\nconstraint maps does.\n\nLet  be any family of continuous linear maps. Their simultaneous\nkernelis weakly closed. Consequently, if  and,\neventually,  for all  simultaneously, then\n for every . If  is weakly closed and norm bounded,\nthen  is weakly compact.The same argument preserves, under weak limits, incompressibility, zero mean,\nhomogeneous traces, and other linear PDE side conditions once the relevant\noperators are continuous.\n\nEvery continuous linear map  is continuous from the weak topology of\n to the weak topology of : composing a continuous functional on \nwith  gives a continuous functional on . Hence each kernel is weakly\nclosed, and arbitrary intersections preserve closedness. A weak limit that is\neventually in this intersection remains in it. Finally, intersecting with a\nweakly closed bounded set preserves closedness and boundedness, so Hilbert\nweak compactness applies.\n\nA weak limit produced by compactness comes with no size information of its\nown. The evaluation property supplies it, and in the direct method this keeps\nthe minimizer inside the same ball as the minimizing sequence.\n\nIn any real normed space, if  and eventually\n, thenThis is the bounded-net form of weak lower semicontinuity of the norm;\nneither completeness nor reflexivity is needed.\n\nHahn–Banach supplies a continuous linear functional  with\n and . The eventual norm\nbound gives . Weak convergence passes this inequality\nto the limit, yielding . The norming-functional\nstatement also covers .\n\nNorm can be lost in a weak limit, as an orthonormal sequence tending weakly to\nzero shows. The Radon–Riesz property says that this loss is the only\nobstruction to strong convergence.\n\nWeak convergence in a real Hilbert space, together with convergence of the\nnorms to the norm of the weak limit, implies strong convergence:We use this Radon–Riesz property of Hilbert spaces when an energy identity\nupgrades a weakly obtained limit or trace to norm convergence.\n\nExpand the squared distance:Weak convergence gives\n, while the additional\nnorm hypothesis gives . All three terms are now\ncontrolled, and their limit is zero. For an orthonormal sequence the mixed\nterm still converges, but the squared norms stay at one; that is precisely\nthe missing hypothesis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method\u0009Weak Compactness for the Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.4.1. Weak compactness toolkit","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Weak-Compactness-for-the-Direct-Method--Weak-compactness-toolkit"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces":{"contents":"Hölder spaces record a quantitative form of continuity:\n is controlled by a power . They are the natural\ntarget of Morrey's supercritical Sobolev embeddings and the regularity class\nin which many compactness and nonlinear arguments recover pointwise control.\n\nFor example, on ,\n\n\n\nThus  is Hölder with exponent  and constant one.\nIt is not Lipschitz there, because\n is unbounded as .\nThe exponent describes how quickly oscillation shrinks with distance; it\ndoes not assert that a classical derivative exists or is bounded. A Hölder\nseminorm also ignores additive constants, which is why the bounded Hölder\nnorm includes the size of the function itself.\n\nThree layers must be kept separate. Mathlib's HolderWith C γ f is a\nquantitative predicate with a specified constant ; MemHolder γ f says\nthat some finite constant works; and HolderSpace.C0GammaOn Ω γ is the\ncompleted normed space used as a target of Sobolev embeddings. The first two\nlayers and the least-constant construction come from Mathlib; PDEAtlas adds\nonly the completed space of bounded real-valued Hölder functions.\n\nThe domain parameter  is an arbitrary metric type. For a geometric\nset , one takes the subtype with its inherited metric; no\nopenness, measurability, Euclidean structure, or boundedness of the set is\nneeded to define the space. The functions themselves are required to be\nbounded. Thus, on an unbounded domain this is the bounded Hölder space, not the\nspace of all Hölder functions.\n\nUnlike a Sobolev graph space, this type stores an actual pointwise\nrepresentative. Equality is pointwise, evaluation is meaningful at every\npoint, and restriction is literal function restriction. A later Morrey\nembedding therefore has two obligations: construct an element of this\nrepresentative space and prove that it agrees almost everywhere with the\noriginal Sobolev coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings","header":"4.3.3. Hölder Spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Bounds-and-Models/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models":{"contents":"\n\nThe exponential in the figure returns us to the heat profile that opened the\nchapter. Here the question is quantitative: how far can a polynomial lie from\nthe function anywhere on the stated interval? Increasing the degree can\nreduce that error, but the final zero-width band replaces the polynomial by\nthe exponential itself: the same inequality with the exponential as its own\nmodel and error zero. The plotted coordinates illustrate the estimate; the\ncertificates supply proofs.\n\nThe shared semantic vocabulary is intentionally small:\n\n* ApproxOn f g s ε means\n for every ;* BoundOn f s c means  throughout ;* EnclosedOn f s lo hi means\n throughout ;* ResidualBound r s ε means \nthroughout .\n\nThese are ordinary propositions in the language of approximation theory. The\ncertificate records ApproxCert, ResidualCert, BoundCert, and\nEnclosureCert store the functions, domain, and numerical data of one of them\ntogether with a sound field proving it; a QuadratureCert does the same for\nthe error of an interval integral. Checked certificates compose through\nordinary Lean theorems.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.7. Certified Bounds and Models","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Bounds-and-Models/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models"}});